      "side": 1,
      "message": "This relaxes the bound from a literal SINT32 to PLDM_SENSOR_DATA_SIZE_MAX, which this change redefines as SINT64, so tags 6 and 7 now reach the extraction below.\n\ndecode_get_sensor_reading_resp() is LIBPLDM_ABI_STABLE and takes present_reading as a plain uint8_t *. Existing callers had no reason to allocate more than four bytes, because a 64-bit reading could not be decoded before this change. The unguarded 64-bit case now added to pldm_msgbuf_extract_sensor_value() then writes eight bytes into that buffer, so a responder that reports sensorDataSize \u003d uint64 causes an out-of-bounds write in the caller.\n\nThis looks like the same issue Andrew raised on 84665 on 2026-01-29:\n\n  \"This must be guarded to prevent the 64-bit access. Existing users may only have passed a 32-bit buffer. This may cause an out-of-bounds write.\"\n\nKeeping the literal SINT32 bound here - and adding a separate 64-bit capable entry point, the way decode_numeric_sensor_data_to_value() is introduced for the event path - would avoid it.",
      "revId": "817cfb8f785c55f10f7c21d3876ef3a5e28ef768",
      "serverId": "adbd0a64-1f21-4d83-b585-671fe73cb6e4"
    },
